<h1><hr class="Journal2">COORS BREWERY TOUR - Monday, November 22, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041122/DSCN0170.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041122/Small/DSCN0170.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041122/DSCN0205.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041122/Small/DSCN0205.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041122/DSCN0339.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041122/Small/DSCN0339.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041122/DSCN0367.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041122/Small/DSCN0367.jpg"></a>
<p>
A real bed at Paul's place in Denver, and a plan for the day: Golden, Colorado, fifteen minutes up the road and home of the Coors brewery.  I'm a sucker for a factory tour, and this one ends in free samples.
<p>
The tour winds past the copper kettles and the packaging lines, with a guide who has answered every question before and still smiles, and finishes in the tasting room as promised.  Afterwards I wander Golden's main street, an old mining town that cleaned up nicely, and find a late lunch.
<p>
Then up Lookout Mountain to Buffalo Bill's grave with the sun going down.  The museum closes before I get there, so I settle for the grave, a cold wind, and the lights of Denver coming on below.
<p>
A gym stop on the way back and an evening at Paul's, travel writing and phone calls.  Tomorrow, weather permitting, Pikes Peak.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041122&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<a id="Ref2"></a>
<br>
<h1><hr class="Journal2">BLOWN PLANS & FINANCIAL ACCOUNTING - Tuesday, November 23, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041123/DSCN0010.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041123/Small/DSCN0010.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041123/DSCN0005.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041123/Small/DSCN0005.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041123/DSCN0007.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041123/Small/DSCN0007.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041123/DSCN0008.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041123/Small/DSCN0008.jpg"></a>
<p>
My plans to drive down to Pikes Peak are scuttled today due to a rather nasty weather forecast for the mountains. Having no backup plans, I decide to take it easy and sort through all the receipts I've accrued so far on the trip. As you can see, it's a bit of a fire hazard. Outside of messing with the receipts, travel writing, and hanging out with Paul, not much else to report.
<p>
As an aside, I do have to admit, it's nice sleeping inside. However, it's best not get too used to this. After I leave Paul's place I'll be sleeping in the van in the freezing cold. Can't say I'm really looking forward to that.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041123&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<a id="Ref3"></a>
<br>
<h1><hr class="Journal2">PIKES PEAK & CLIFF DWELLINGS - Wednesday, November 24, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041124/DSCN0206.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041124/Small/DSCN0206.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041124/DSCN0277.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041124/Small/DSCN0277.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041124/DSCN0336.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041124/Small/DSCN0336.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041124/DSCN0708.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041124/Small/DSCN0708.jpg"></a>
<p>
Yay! Looks like it's a go for checking out Pikes Peak today.
<p>
I head out mid-morning on a relatively short drive of 70 miles to get there. However, I run into a problem when I'm close to the top. I can't go all the way, the last 1,500 feet or so are off limits, boiling down to many, many miles of road and missed vistas. The road is temporary closed due to all the snow removal equipment that's removing the mountain of snow that was dumped here yesterday afternoon.
<p>
I'm forced to reformulate my travel plans and decide to drop into the Manitou Cliff Dwellings & Museum which isn't far away. The Dwellings and Museum go far in painting an interesting picture of the life and times of the Anasazi. I stay till sunset, then head back to Paul's place to relax for the rest of the evening.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041124&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<a id="Ref4"></a>
<br>
<h1><hr class="Journal2">A DENVER THANKSGIVING - Thursday, November 25, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041125/DSCN0064.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041125/Small/DSCN0064.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041125/DSCN0354.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041125/Small/DSCN0354.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041125/DSCN0201.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041125/Small/DSCN0201.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041125/DSCN0219.JPG&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041125/Small/DSCN0219.jpg"></a>
<p>
Today is Thanksgiving. Not only am I thankful for the nice sunny weather, I'm also thankful I'll be spending the holiday with Paul's family this evening for dinner and boardgames. Since dinner isn't till this evening, I feel the need to fill my morning and afternoon with worthwhile sightseeing activities. Since Downtown Denver is just a quick drive from where I'm staying, I decide to make that my tourist destination today.
<p>
If you didn't already know, Denver is called the "Mile High City." Situated exactly one mile in the air (5,280 feet), it's the most populous city in the Rocky Mountains and hosts a diverse economy, great outdoor activities, and a thriving cultural scene. It offers a unique combination of both urban life and mountain living.
<p>
After a quick drive, I find myself on foot walking around the city. I don't have a set agenda, I just wish to wander around looking for interesting sights, and Denver doesn't disappoint. Sights seen include the Colorado State Capitol Building, Denver Union Station, street musicians, street art, a mixure of skyscrapers and old buildings, and more.
<p>
All toured out, evening arrives and I head back to Paul's place. We then drive out to his brother's house for Thanksgiving. The evening is special, filled with great company and great food. Oh yes, that's right, earlier I mentioned boardgames. We played both Life and Monopoly and I lost both games.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041125&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<a id="Ref5"></a>
<br>
<h1><hr class="Journal2">COLORADO GOLD - Friday, November 26, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0238.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0238.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0193.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0193.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0214.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0214.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041126.1/DSCN0234.jpg"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041126.1/Small/DSCN0234.jpg"></a>
<p>
I wake at Paul's for the last time, pack up the van, and say my goodbyes.  Paul's been a great host and Denver's been a great base camp this past week, but it's time to move on.  Today's destination is the Phoenix Gold Mine, a small working mine up a snowy gulch above Idaho Springs, Colorado, less than an hour west of Denver on I-70.
<p>
On the way up I call ahead and get lucky, Al, the owner and operator, is giving tours today.  I join Denise and her father Gordon for a hard-hat walk a few hundred feet into the mountain, where Al points out veins of gold in the rock, lets us swing a pick, and explains how the ore gets crushed, floated and shaken until only the gold is left.  Al has big plans for the place, and it proves to be a really cool tour.
<p>
I follow Denise and Gordon back down to Idaho Springs where I meet the rest of her family over pizza, then hide out from the cold in a coffee house and, after closing time, in the Tommyknocker brewpub.  It's 9 degrees out when I finally leave.  I point the van west through the Eisenhower Tunnel and overnight in Frisco, Colorado, where I'm about to find out what sleeping in the van in single digits is all about.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041126&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<a id="Ref6"></a>
<br>
<h1><hr class="Journal2">TOUR OF VAIL, CO - Saturday, November 27, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041127/DSCN0402.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041127/Small/DSCN0402.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041127/DSCN0293.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041127/Small/DSCN0293.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041127/DSCN0422.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041127/Small/DSCN0422.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041127/DSCN0371.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041127/Small/DSCN0371.jpg"></a>
<p>
I wake in Frisco, Colorado to my first truly cold night in the van, 11 degrees outside and not a whole lot warmer inside.  I slept poorly and wake with a sore throat, so the day's skiing plans get shelved.  Instead I spend a small fortune at Wal-Mart on winter gear, boots, gloves and chains for the van, then head west over Vail Pass on a nearly empty tank.
<p>
Vail, Colorado is a ski village built to look like a Bavarian town, and I spend about an hour and a half walking it in the cold, taking pictures of shops, restaurants, bronze bears and the covered bridge over Gore Creek.  I'm not a shopper, so I don't go into a single store.  The outsides are more interesting anyway.  A good lunch and a snowboard video on the restaurant TV warm me up some.
<p>
The rest of the day is spent hiding from the cold in a coffee shop, travel writing and processing photos.  Come evening I drive down the valley to Avon, then back toward Vail to overnight at the local Wal-Mart.  Tomorrow, if I'm feeling better, I ski.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041127&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<p>
<a id="Ref7"></a>
<br>
<h1><hr class="Journal2">COME SKI WITH ME - Sunday, November 28, 2004<hr class="Journal2"></h1>
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041128/DSCN0144.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041128/Small/DSCN0144.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041128/DSCN0168.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041128/Small/DSCN0168.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041128/DSCN0114.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041128/Small/DSCN0114.jpg"></a>
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=Daily/20041128/DSCN0277.jpg&nocomments=1"><img src="Pics/Daily/20041128/Small/DSCN0277.jpg"></a>
<p>
I wake at the Avon Wal-Mart under a foot of new powder, feeling better than yesterday, and there's no question what today is.  I drive the few miles up to Lionshead, pay $15 to park, $41.50 for rental skis and $49 for a lift ticket, and ride the Eagle Bahn gondola to the top of Vail Mountain in a snowstorm.
<p>
Born Free is my run, an intermediate that comes right back down to the gondola.  I ski it four times.  The first run the legs remember, the third the sun comes out over the Gore Range, and by the fourth the powder is skied off to ice and moguls and I'm wiped out.  I take a few pictures through my new orange goggles so you can see the day the way I did.
<p>
Rentals returned, a Subway sandwich, a beer at a Lionshead bar until they close early for Sunday, then back to the Wal-Mart McDonald's to soak up some heat.  My sinuses are full and I'm running a temperature.  Tomorrow I head for Black Canyon and Montrose, and if I'm still sick, a hotel room.
<p>
<a href="index.php?action=DailyEvents&date=20041128&page=1" title="Travel Log">Description & Pics</a>
<p>
